,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(1,2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497610 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5c268641836391c9ca70392071670c6c6948340d8a2521ac8729043e924f934.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}2x + y = -15\\\\x - y = 0\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/00/009307e974ef19d7513437feecd23f2bef97978d5c5412a7f2afa8f12460dc49.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(-5,-5)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(-5,-5)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497616 :question/problem "![](images/23/23f47d3d6f9cd1c1a21eaf57ca8c0770edffe071b585849dd3413649e4f6ba60.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}2x - y = -1\\\\x + y = -2\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/bc/bc7329c1db1e5cad351bf328ecb08058676b773b25d89f7bb36114f7268c2463.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(-1,-1)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(-1,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497622 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc4e519836578d03d666361be248aba6fa132afabce4dafe67e90ff046dc6ce2.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}x - y = 3\\\\y =2x - 5\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/83/83f3796c9fba5edb2f2ee5ff5d37e8f9226bbdbb476f865a5ff54d6c9924cb87.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(2,-1)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(2,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497625 :question/problem "![](images/04/0410474fb1fb667f14cf7903303a8883a6d18ec9ab485399c10ed923121358e0.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}2x - y = -1\\\\2x + y = -11\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/c3/c353bbc95fa96153fcc4807029065d3986a25a3fab05b42e39b45a97ffb07824.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(-3,-5)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(-3,-5)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497628 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e888ba1f184d8cb33b8e7f15849a7742a34bd440881661382c530355f15f0ad3.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}2x - y = -9\\\\2x + y = -3\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/39/39da4557e51b930edee9270bf81092adb0af11b261c983521606b0dee95eefae.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(-3,3)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(-3,3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497639 :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebfb726e49a550c0453d6fbfd6c4bab15fbbc23416ec276e7514d0f7db0b2945.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}x + y = -1\\\\2x + y = -3\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/85/853ad66abafc98e64a9918d25394eaeb285a867b821c430205ba890d11914ad5.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(-2,1)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(-2,1)$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138084 :question/math-academy-id "e-13130" :question/problem "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400).$ So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138783 :question/math-academy-id "q-45165" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d5e3ef2393a00fc74c91ebc44f71d553e641d0e1bca21bf783252cbafcca84c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139116 :question/math-academy-id "q-59886" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b91b1093a485c671cb5e7566498a1e2b95452d9b92ea87e478696ff8c9ace0ff.png)\n\nTwo trucks, Truck $A$ and Truck $B$, are carrying shipments between distribution centers. The graph above shows the distances (in miles) of Truck $A$ and Truck $B$ from one of the centers as a function of time (in hours). At what time are Truck $A$ and Truck $B$ the same distance from the center?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495118 :question/math-academy-id "q-102519" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a9b092dbb1a08d4cfc877ab4dc62e68ca90663bc87b810354e4973a90ff8d9a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the altitude of airplane $A$ and airplane $B$ after they have taken off, one after the other. After how many minutes will both planes be at the same altitude?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to find the point of intersection of the two graphs.\n\n![](images/6c/6c0b061e315f7470aa16c034b92592306ca2f56885af03e3e5d6646619c8f02f.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(12,20).$ So, it takes $12$ minutes for both planes to get to the same altitude (of $20,000\\,\\text{ft}$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495144 :question/math-academy-id "q-237989" :question/problem "![](images/45/4534ed02abf6740df78c3285292e7235391bc76352e16cfdcd2a149dfc7046c4.png)\n\nWater is being pumped from tank $A$ to tank $B.$ The graph above shows the amount of water (in liters) in each tank as a function of time (in minutes). At what time do tanks $A$ and $B$ contain the same amount of water?\n\nThe tanks contain the same amount of water after{{field-1}}minutes." :question/worked-solution "The tanks contain the same amount when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/23/23efe932ef71f93f3a455a93bda45ded7e44b059853308f6d760a4df4bc7d349.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,15).$ So, the tanks contain the same amount ($15\\,\\text{L}$) of water after $t=4$ minutes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497499 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6c7f3f028fc6e9feed9b8091079869a217f469af727751b75d217ae28a99151.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?" :question/worked-solution "The cars have the same speed when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/8f/8ff48c2f5ff89f861604a0ca82b5c053337360f448466b61d5a2b134cf520297.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(90,80)$. So, both cars have the same speed ($80\\,\\textrm{km/h}$) at a distance of $90$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497507 :question/problem "![](images/86/86efe26cf45e9b659b21460c530999db4eb6aed9b0473f06d28aad0e21dd76b3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?" :question/worked-solution "The cars have the same speed when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/ea/eab1a45d7f476950909440727f6ca69a7d4f1128a66a4cc10d6c6efe7e07facc.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(50,60)$. So, both cars have the same speed ($60\\,\\textrm{km/h}$) at a distance of $50$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497515 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd6eaf922ab844494e47fc81b4602c9289192f565021dd01aea9f214b4835967.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/b0/b0272f7b4a39180737a4c52ccb3548ff2630336cfee91042ea432b7b412850ff.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(9,500)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($500\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=9$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497516 :question/problem "![](images/45/45b2b3239b2f9fd572693519a93d6fd4e1920b0d4dbccf6261a29245d89d8f0b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/a5/a5ff74710305609ff41802c8e83ac3eed63d325daabf3cba9394f7884c2a2636.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,300)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($300\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497517 :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8cf548ffa7d5109031f7a13611aecb941802ea0d1c2bf33779a47472ba9e910.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/95/950fdfa29db712593739cf8570fcf9cb16261a8c361ba9462ea3902a86261dd9.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(9,500)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($500\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=9$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497520 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c069163e293c0ffd7d37b869ef49e828048a51361a863a99c4049e7677759555.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?" :question/worked-solution "The cars have the same speed when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/62/62888dfe3e92b902c3dc816b249d322bf70aae157921eb640f06e75b4584a9aa.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(70,30)$. So, both cars have the same speed ($30\\,\\textrm{km/h}$) at a distance of $70$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497521 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f96feaa2fb67b7e8e91fb6fd8e25a4b502ee9f6e627de9c037bb542fabbe8f5e.png)\n\nTwo trucks, Truck $A$ and Truck $B$, are carrying shipments between distribution centers. The graph above shows the distances (in miles) of Truck $A$ and Truck $B$ from one of the centers as a function of time (in hours). At what time are Truck $A$ and Truck $B$ the same distance from the center?" :question/worked-solution "The trucks are the same distance from the center when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/96/96b0c8f88a657c72a77f5efb904b05ea641728f861eab4af3079282707f8a3b7.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,200)$. So, both trucks are the same distance ($200\\,\\textrm{miles}$) from the center at $t=4$ hours."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497528 :question/problem "![](images/48/48853f991ecf0deb0f5833e0a00763e70fc262b488162ab6e31c14e8206c829c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/b7/b7b37065e8c66152c2a9c83f48bba7b7c73dccbdf1b48d3f89e93c49c8339e65.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(9,600)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($600\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=9$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497538 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5779eb061e64a8cf02a432e1d48b0b2045d7734531931a2380c5e5719f062bb.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the altitude of airplane $A$ and airplane $B$ after they have taken off, one after the other. After how many minutes will both planes be at the same altitude?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to find the point of intersection of the two graphs.\n\n![](images/80/800b3b0869fa682e4dfefec48e1240bccd0ae5818a7b7e5c9752167b2e694127.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(15,20).$ So, it takes $15$ minutes for both planes to get to the same altitude (of $20,000\\,\\text{ft}$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497557 :question/problem "![](images/f6/f685b633fc464f19815dba9ea2a8f74b8086465d8bfd715e7c1a026712d3a91a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?" :question/worked-solution "The cars have the same speed when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/65/65c206eac975899b492b503659288650d8d32bdd71a4c56bba8f40508c9ee463.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(30,50)$. So, both cars have the same speed ($50\\,\\textrm{km/h}$) at a distance of $30$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497560 :question/problem "![](images/49/49e498ca87f38c193c5d080e539899d569ff285a293b823390dac185c908ea6d.png)\n\nWater is being pumped from tank $A$ to tank $B.$ The graph above shows the amount of water (in liters) in each tank as a function of time (in minutes). At what time do tanks $A$ and $B$ contain the same amount of water?\n\nThe tanks contain the same amount of water after{{field-1}}minutes." :question/worked-solution "The tanks contain the same amount when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/fe/fe2a095cf5f4deee4fea923dc4c72bbbfe8b4917fb381a7b23b845a3e4deae24.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(2,15).$ So, the tanks contain the same amount ($15\\,\\text{L}$) of water after $t=2$ minutes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497562 :question/problem "![](images/86/86eacea79b1ee9cfd132ad68dfb4e52b2e5ee9725ac82ca8bb28d8d853f9777d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the speed of cars $A$ and $B$ as a function of the distance that they traveled during a brake test. At which distance, in meters, did both cars reach the same speed?" :question/worked-solution "The cars have the same speed when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/31/31fed6da917a9bc33cac4f50dd09c62688298283d0b9c91b75bd4ac74776fbb7.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(50,70)$. So, both cars have the same speed ($70\\,\\textrm{km/h}$) at a distance of $50$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497571 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c77984914ba2b8d2812b3710a4adf81812972dba95e88ac2a20158e04649fd8e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/f3/f3ab18f302a6120174d62ccc06f9b1139f01dc2af370b4bd5cc55cada0e8dbbb.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(7,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=7$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503674 :question/math-academy-id "q-166758" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba84a222a38abb930dbf0dde24479abcbfb3ccdca4f50beec0792e90e212d0b0.png)\n\nSam left home to go to the community park at the same time that Mary left the community park to go home. Both Mary's and Sam's distances from home are shown in the graph above. Based on the graph, we conclude that:\n\nMary and Sam met$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes since they both started moving.\n\nThey met at a distance of$\\,$ \n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from their home." :question/worked-solution "Mary and Sam meet when they are at the same distance from home. At this moment, their graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/8a/8afd4e9a8374ebe1e35200f6172e7fac9ef6c132d84e532c3ac44c25cb838a0d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(11,300).$ So, Mary and her brother met $11$ minutes since they started moving at a distance of $300\\,\\text{m}$ away from their home."}] :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"}
